  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and tranquil weather will persist through at \nleast Thursday with a gradual warming trend. \n\nAn expansive area of high pressure will remain in control through \nThursday resulting in a stretch of nice mid-September days.\n\nFor tonight, with the surface high lingering near the area it will \nresult in another night of mainly clear skies and light winds \nleading to good radiational cooling conditions. It won't be quite as \ncool as last night in most places but generally expecting most areas \nto get down into the 50s with some upper 40s possible in the \nPoconos. \n\nWednesday through Thursday will feature a gradual warming trend as \nhigh pressure slips off to our south as a weak system moves in north \nof the region by Thursday. This will result in a SW flow developing \nwith warm air advection. Generally expect highs in the 80s with dew \npoints also creeping up through this period as well. Both days \nshould be largely free of any precipitation though by late day \nThursday an approaching cold front from the north could result \nin a few showers affecting the Lehigh Valley into the southern \nPoconos. POPs though are only around 20-30 percent. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Somewhat unsettled weather will be possible at\ntimes late this week and next weekend as multiple systems move \nthrough. However at this point significant impacts due not \nappear likely.\n\nA cold front still looks to move southward through the area Thursday \nnight into Friday and this could result in some showers. Guidance \nhad been backing off on precipitation with this but some of our \nlatest guidance is a bit more robust once again with showers moving \nthrough. The bottom line though is that with the timing mainly at \nnight and mostly just showers expected (probability for thunder is \nlow) this should not be an impactful system. \n\nA couple showers could linger into Friday over our southern zones \nbut otherwise it should be a partly to mostly sunny day with \ntemperatures cooling slightly compared to Thursday in the wake of \nthe front. \n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, the aformentioned front looks to \neventually move back to the north as a warm front late Saturday into \nSaturday night. The front may then linger near the area Sunday. As \nthis occurs, moisture looks to surge back northward towards the area \nahead of a tough moving through the Great Plains. This will bring \nincreasing chances for showers and perhaps a few storms by late \nSaturday night and especially on Sunday which looks to be the wetter \nof the two weekend days. POPs for Sunday range from around 30% south \nup to 60% north. \n\nMedium range guidance shows the unsettled period potentially \ncontinuing into early next week with multiple waves of low pressure \nmoving through. Still over 5 days out, so things could change with \nthe forecast and it is a bit early to get into specific impacts. \nHowever, there is no real overwhelming signal in both deterministic \nand ensembles of any major impacts at this time with these systems.",
